        {
          "dimension": "Centralization",
          "level": "Medium",
          "note": "Corporate co-sponsors (Google Cloud, Coinbase Ventures) structurally steer winning project architectures toward their own infrastructure and APIs, concentrating dependency risk in ecosystems that emerge from these events.",
          "source": "https://www.encodeclub.com/programmes/kites-hackathon-ai-agentic-economy"
        },
        {
          "dimension": "Regulatory",
          "level": "High",
          "note": "Hackathons explicitly building autonomous AI agents that negotiate, pay, and transact onchain without human sign-off sit in uncharted regulatory territory for both securities law and AML compliance.",
          "source": "https://www.galaxy.com/insights/research/x402-ai-agents-crypto-payments"
        },
        {
          "dimension": "Liquidity",
          "level": "Low",
          "note": "Prize pools ($200K–$2.5M) are large enough to attract builders but too small to create systemic liquidity risk; the primary concern is winner projects launching tokens prematurely to capitalize on hackathon visibility.",
          "source": "https://x.com/solana/status/2048020834862981522"
        },
        {
          "dimension": "Market",
          "level": "Medium",
          "note": "Hackathon-born projects frequently launch tokens or raise seed rounds within weeks of winning, creating speculative price spikes disconnected from mainnet readiness and long-term utility.",
